          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly avows Cordelia's love for Lear. Its contextual qualification limits the amount or manner of love claimed but does not remove its truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Cordelia explicitly invokes an existing filial bond and the duties she owes Lear as his daughter. She presents loving, obeying, and honouring him as duties returned to him, rather than making a new promise. Cordelia is the obligated party and Lear is the recipient of those filial duties.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The expression is represented as a controlled, carefully delimited answer rather than a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia says she cannot simply pour her heart into speech and measures the avowal as “no more nor less.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a corrective boundary-setting response: Cordelia refuses Lear's invitation to maximize or exaggerate her declaration competitively, insisting on a limited and proportionate formulation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to his distribution of the kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia is pressed to speak again and gives the focal avowal as a deliberately qualified account of a daughter's love and duty.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughter-to-father bond comprising love, obedience, honour, care, and duty; she does not clearly undertake a new future commitment. Lear does not accept her formulation as an adequate answer: he tells her to “Mend your speech a little” and warns that it may harm her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The passages strongly support avowal, filial obligation, and actual affection through Cordelia's speech and asides. They do not independently settle every question about her inner state beyond this represented moment, nor do they establish a newly negotiated undertaking with terms requiring Lear's acceptance.\"}"
